WHAT: give KConfig a WASI path in four places -- getuid(), two QProcess
      helper launches, QDateTime timezone round-tripping -- and skip the
      kconf_update / kreadconfig standalone executables.
WHY:  KConfig is where the whole KDE settings story lives, so every one of
      these had to be answered rather than dodged:
        * kconfiginibackendreader_p.h:239 "no member named getuid in the global
          namespace; did you mean getpid?". wasi-libc has no getuid because a
          WASI component has no user identity -- there is exactly one principal
          inside a wk node and everything in its vfs belongs to it. Upstream is
          asking "does this file belong to me, or am I writing into someone
          else s?", and here the answer is unconditionally "mine", so the WASI
          branch takes the owned-by-user consequence: preserve the existing
          file s permissions.
        * kconfig.cpp:29 `#include <QProcess>` (FEATURE_process=OFF). Two uses,
          both "shell out to a helper binary": kconf_update --check to migrate
          stale config files, and kdialog --msgbox to tell the user their
          config is read-only. reparseConfiguration() still runs in the first
          case, and upstream already treats a missing kdialog as "warn
          nowhere", so the second is a path a stock desktop takes too. A wk
          node config starts empty in its own vfs, so there is nothing for
          kconf_update to migrate anyway.
        * kconfiggroup.cpp:360 and :1009 -- QTimeZone(QByteArray) and
          QDateTime::timeZone(), gone with FEATURE_timezone=OFF. Consequence:
          a QDateTime entry round-trips WITHOUT its zone, i.e. as local time.
          Config files written by this build and read by a desktop KDE would
          lose the zone field. Worth knowing, harmless for a self-contained
          node with no zone database in the first place.
        * src/CMakeLists.txt -- kconf_update, kreadconfig and kwriteconfig are
          standalone executables that only make sense on the machine whose
          config is being migrated or inspected. Cross-built to wasm they are
          components nobody can invoke, and kconf_update.cpp:16 fails on
          QProcess regardless.
UPSTREAM: the QProcess and QTimeZone hunks are as-is (both are real Qt feature
      switches with sensible fallbacks already in the file). The getuid hunk is
      not-as-is -- upstream would want a HAVE_GETUID configure check next to
      the existing Q_OS_WIN one.
diff --git a/src/CMakeLists.txt b/src/CMakeLists.txt
index ab5ddc8..38bee2f 100644
--- a/src/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -6,8 +6,16 @@ if(KCONFIG_USE_QML)
     add_subdirectory(qml)
 endif()
 add_subdirectory(kconfig_compiler)
-add_subdirectory(kconf_update)
-add_subdirectory(kreadconfig)
+# kconf_update, kreadconfig and kwriteconfig are standalone EXECUTABLES that
+# only make sense on the machine whose config is being migrated or inspected.
+# Cross-built for wasm32-wasip2 they would be three more components nobody can
+# invoke -- a wk node runs one program. kconf_update.cpp:16 also fails outright
+# on `#include <QProcess>` (FEATURE_process=OFF). Skipping the directories is
+# the honest version of "these are not part of this port".
+if(NOT CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
+    add_subdirectory(kconf_update)
+    add_subdirectory(kreadconfig)
+endif()
 
 ecm_qt_install_logging_categories(
     EXPORT KCONFIG
diff --git a/src/core/CMakeLists.txt b/src/core/CMakeLists.txt
index aebaa47..439f2f3 100644
--- a/src/core/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/core/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -50,10 +50,19 @@ ecm_generate_export_header(KF6ConfigCore
     EXCLUDE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE_AND_AT ${EXCLUDE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE_AND_AT}
 )
 
-target_compile_definitions(KF6ConfigCore
-    PRIVATE
-        KCONF_UPDATE_INSTALL_LOCATION="${KDE_INSTALL_FULL_LIBEXECDIR_KF}/$<TARGET_FILE_NAME:KF6::kconf_update>"
-)
+# The generator expression needs the KF6::kconf_update TARGET to exist, and
+# src/CMakeLists.txt does not add that directory on WASI (see the comment
+# there). The macro is only read by the QProcess call in KConfig::checkUpdate,
+# which is itself #if QT_CONFIG(process) -- so on this platform it names a
+# binary that can neither be built nor run, and the honest value is nothing.
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
+    target_compile_definitions(KF6ConfigCore PRIVATE KCONF_UPDATE_INSTALL_LOCATION="")
+else()
+    target_compile_definitions(KF6ConfigCore
+        PRIVATE
+            KCONF_UPDATE_INSTALL_LOCATION="${KDE_INSTALL_FULL_LIBEXECDIR_KF}/$<TARGET_FILE_NAME:KF6::kconf_update>"
+    )
+endif()
 
 target_include_directories(KF6ConfigCore
     INTERFACE "$<INSTALL_INTERFACE:${KDE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR_KF}/KConfig;${KDE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR_KF}/KConfigCore>"
